WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.552
A bakery customer paid 68.37 for a party order, then brought most of it back.

2
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.741
Two staff members, at two tills, each press refund 60.00, in the same second.

3
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.641
The rule is that refunds never add up to more than the payment.

4
00:00:17.641 --> 00:00:19.956
So only one of these should go through.

5
00:00:19.956 --> 00:00:21.856
How does the database make sure?

6
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:25.383
The habit from SQL databases is separate records.

7
00:00:25.383 --> 00:00:32.287
A payment is one row, each refund is a row in another table, and the screen joins them back together.

8
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.104
MongoDB, a free database program, can do the same.

9
00:00:36.104 --> 00:00:44.548
It stores each record as a document: names and values inside curly braces, shaped like JSON, a common text format.

10
00:00:44.548 --> 00:00:47.140
A set of documents is a collection.

11
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:54.157
So version one: a payments collection, and a refunds collection where each refund holds its payment's id.

12
00:00:54.157 --> 00:00:55.855
That's called referencing.

13
00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:57.880
The screen now needs two reads.

14
00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:00.876
Each till checks the rule, then writes.

15
00:01:00.876 --> 00:01:04.354
Till one reads the refunds: none yet, so 60.00 fits.

16
00:01:04.354 --> 00:01:08.100
Till two reads at the same moment: none yet, 60.00 fits.

17
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:09.076
Both write.

18
00:01:09.076 --> 00:01:11.912
What does the bakery refund in total?

19
00:01:11.912 --> 00:01:13.139
Pause and guess.

20
00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:19.021
120.00, on a payment of 68.37.

21
00:01:19.021 --> 00:01:22.737
The bakery's accountant would like a word.

22
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:29.086
The check and the write were two steps, on two documents, and the other till slipped in between.

23
00:01:29.086 --> 00:01:37.527
The usual cure is a lock around everything, or a multi-document transaction: a group of writes that happen together, or not at all.

24
00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:39.763
Think of the paper receipt instead.

25
00:01:39.763 --> 00:01:44.388
Write each refund on the receipt itself, with a running total at the bottom.

26
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:49.224
To write on it, you have to hold it, and only one person can hold it at a time.

27
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:55.401
Till one takes the receipt, sees nothing refunded, writes 60.00, and the total becomes 60.00.

28
00:01:55.567 --> 00:02:01.210
Till two gets it next, and 60 more on top of 60 is more than 68.37.

29
00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:02.641
Nothing to write.

30
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:09.399
The refunds collection was a box of loose slips: anyone can check the box, and anyone can drop a slip in.

31
00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:12.074
Version two does what the receipt does.

32
00:02:12.074 --> 00:02:17.281
The refunds go inside the payment's document, in a list, next to a running total.

33
00:02:17.281 --> 00:02:18.824
That's called embedding.

34
00:02:18.933 --> 00:02:27.772
And in MongoDB, a write to one document is atomic: it happens completely or not at all, and no other write slips in halfway.

35
00:02:27.933 --> 00:02:31.280
So one update does the check and the change together.

36
00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.333
It changes the payment only if the total plus this refund is at most the amount.

37
00:02:36.333 --> 00:02:39.175
Then it adds the refund and raises the total.

38
00:02:39.333 --> 00:02:40.799
Two refunds of 60.00.

39
00:02:40.799 --> 00:02:43.591
How many documents does each one change?

40
00:02:43.591 --> 00:02:44.708
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:49.391
One, then zero.

42
00:02:49.533 --> 00:02:53.340
The second's filter no longer matches, so it changes nothing.

43
00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.712
No error, and no lock you had to take.

44
00:02:55.867 --> 00:02:58.391
And the screen gets everything in one read.

45
00:02:58.567 --> 00:03:00.657
So should everything go inside?

46
00:03:00.657 --> 00:03:00.859
No.

47
00:03:00.859 --> 00:03:05.038
Embed what's read together, changed together, and stays small.

48
00:03:05.038 --> 00:03:07.937
A payment has a handful of refunds at most.

49
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:12.957
Reference what grows without limit, like an account's payments over the years.

50
00:03:12.957 --> 00:03:17.628
A document can be at most 16 megabytes, and a receipt can't be a mile long.

51
00:03:17.867 --> 00:03:28.079
And when two things truly can't share a document, like a transfer between two accounts, MongoDB has multi-document transactions, since version 4.0.

52
00:03:28.079 --> 00:03:32.664
Its own docs say they usually cost more than a one-document write.

53
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.511
So, two tills, one payment.

54
00:03:34.511 --> 00:03:40.216
Put what must change together inside one document, and one guarded write settles the race.

55
00:03:40.633 --> 00:03:41.870
One quick check.

56
00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:46.509
After the first refund, a third till tries a refund of 8.37.

57
00:03:46.509 --> 00:03:47.978
Does it go through?

58
00:03:47.978 --> 00:03:49.215
Pause and think.

59
00:03:52.433 --> 00:03:52.794
Yes.

60
00:03:52.794 --> 00:03:58.201
60.00 plus 8.37 is exactly 68.37, and the rule says at most.

61
00:03:58.201 --> 00:04:03.248
One document changed, and the payment is fully refunded.
